 ## check.sh
 
-### §check-fails-open — the check never blocks a commit because of its own failure
-- Since: 2026-09-11
-- Decision: `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`. Every internal error still ends in `exit 0`; the script only warns.
-- Forbidden: `set -e` or `set -o errexit`, and any exit status other than 0.
-- Reason: with `set -e`, a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file aborted the check and blocked every commit in
-  the repository for a day (2026-09-10). The check is advisory by design.
+### §check-fails-closed — a failure inside the check aborts it with a non-zero exit
+- Since: 2026-10-22
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. Any failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit and its error on stderr.
+  Large staged files still only warn.
+- Forbidden: dropping `-e` (`errexit`), or otherwise letting an internal failure end in exit 0.
+- Reason: maintainer decision (2026-10-22). The fail-open check hid its own breakage, so it could stop working
+  without anyone finding out.
+- Note: under `-e`, the file loop must not end in a bare `[ test ] && cmd`. A false test becomes the loop's status,
+  and with `pipefail` it aborts the check whenever the last staged file is under the limit.
+- Rejected: §check-fails-open (2026-09-11 – 2026-10-22), `set -uo pipefail` with every error ending in `exit 0`. It
+  was adopted after a `set -e` version aborted on a `wc` failure for a staged-then-deleted file and blocked every
+  commit for a day (2026-09-10). The loop skips paths that are no longer regular files (`[ -f "$f" ] || continue`).
